SYM-3 of Symmetry ARC

 

Gauge Inconsistency, Global Response, Inflow, Matching, and Normal-Form Closure in the Standard ModelA Fixed-Target Anomaly Reference for the Standard Model Symmetry Arc

Quantum field theory uses the word “anomaly” for several physically distinct phenomena: failure of a dynamical gauge symmetry at the quantum level, anomalous nonconservation of a meaningful global current, obstruction to gauging a global symmetry, topology-sensitive determinant phases, mixed-background noninvariance, bulk–boundary inflow, ultraviolet–infrared matching constraints, and renormalized trace identities.

This volume develops a fixed-target classification of these phenomena within the Standard Model symmetry programme. It distinguishes their physical loci, causal ancestry, obstruction types, repair mechanisms, presentation freedoms, and target-update boundaries rather than collapsing them into a single exact/broken or conserved/nonconserved label.

The analysis is formulated under the Admissibility–Standing–Reference–Irreversibility kernel. A determinate anomaly claim already requires fixed identity, lawful comparison and transformation, consequence-bearing use, and irreversible fixation. Conventional anomaly theory then supplies the target-specific currents, measures, determinant phases, descent classes, index data, global obstructions, inflow relations, matching conditions, and renormalized operator identities.

The closed SYM-3 family contains ten reference-complete anomaly targets:



the one-generation Standard Model local gauge-anomaly ledger;

vector/axial descent with Bardeen structure;

a symmetric vectorlike six-Weyl theory;

the ordinary and newer SU(2)SU(2) global anomalies;

the massless three-flavor QCD U(1)AU(1)_A anomaly;

electroweak B+LB+L;

a four-dimensional Weyl boundary with five-dimensional inflow;

three-flavor QCD gauged-Wess–Zumino–Witten matching;

the massless-QCD trace anomaly in a fixed MS‾\overline{\mathrm{MS}} operator target.


Across these targets, the construction contains:



63 independently derived role positions;

590 role-blind physical anomaly candidates;

4,412 evaluated same-fiber pairs;

91,695 complete candidate maps;

91,597 physical-compatibility rejections;

98 surviving compatible objects;

one lawful normal-form isomorphism class for each closed target.


The volume establishes a common anomaly grammar distinguishing:



gauge inconsistency from anomalous global response;

local descent from global determinant and bordism obstruction;

consistent, covariant, and Bardeen-shifted current representatives;

counterterm redistribution from genuine physical cancellation;

anomaly inflow from retrospective repair;

’t Hooft anomaly matching from uniqueness of infrared realization;

trace anomalies from gauge anomalies;

closed anomaly targets from generalized, higher-group, non-invertible, and global-form frontier interfaces.


The resulting normal-form groupoid retains nontrivial presentation morphisms, automorphisms, and stabilizers within a unique isomorphism class. Public anomaly coordinates and repair claims are admitted only when they preserve the fixed target and its invariant physical content.

The volume also provides explicit interfaces for later symmetry modules. Target-relative anomaly records are transported to SYM-4 through SYM-10 under target-preserving rules. Global-form, line-operator, monopole, higher-group, and non-invertible anomaly inventories remain explicit SYM-9 frontier interfaces, while whole-arc candidate exhaustion remains reserved for SYM-11.

The result does not claim that anomaly cancellation uniquely derives the Standard Model, fixes numerical couplings, or determines a unique infrared phase. It is complete only for the declared target and constructor grammars, and the executable audit layer does not rederive imported continuum results such as determinant-line theory, bordism classification, or renormalized quantum-field-theoretic theorems.

Principal contributions



A fixed-target anomaly-status calculus for the Standard Model symmetry arc.

A typed separation of gauge, global, mixed, gravitational, topological, inflow, matching, and trace anomalies.

A distinction between physical obstruction and presentation-dependent current or counterterm choices.

A local anomaly-polynomial, Wess–Zumino, and relative BRST descent framework.

A global-anomaly treatment using determinant lines, eta invariants, bordism, and faithful global form.

A mixed-anomaly and discrete-anomaly classification.

An explicit ’t Hooft anomaly and infrared-matching boundary.

A bulk–boundary inflow and repair-relation analysis.

A generalized, higher-group, and non-invertible anomaly frontier interface.

A trace-anomaly and renormalization-group transport handoff.

Exact Standard Model perturbative and global anomaly ledgers.

A reproducible finite normal-form and compatibility audit architecture.

Overview

Complete Certificate Closure is the terminal FEP-1.0 capstone of the gravity–quantum unification ARC.

The preceding standalone modules discharge the constructive obligations left open by Structural Unification of Einstein and Schrödinger Dynamics:



Module A constructs the operational metric–source attachment.

Module B derives gravitational and inertial mass from one conserved Casimir charge.

Module C constructs the common upstream continuation and proves the Einstein and Schrödinger projection intertwinings.

Module D completes the exact target fibres and proves unique lawful occupancy modulo certificate-preserving equivalence.


The capstone imports these accepted endpoints and proves that they form one dependency-faithful, interface-complete realization certificate.

Its task is not to replace the standalone proofs or manufacture missing occupants by integration. It establishes that the independently proved components retain their precise theorem content when assembled, share the same physical references, close every required interface, and jointly realize the frozen FEP-1.0 target.

Conceptual Significance

The capstone completes the transition from common-origin structure to certified joint realization.

The ARC begins from the claim that determinate physics already presupposes admissibility, standing, reference, and irreversible fixation. Within one admissible physical interior, Einstein and Schrödinger dynamics emerge as distinct first-response projections. The later modules then construct the structures required to turn that common origin into a complete realization:

one admissible interior⟶one operational source⟶one conserved mass charge⟶one upstream continuation\text{one admissible interior} \longrightarrow \text{one operational source} \longrightarrow \text{one conserved mass charge} \longrightarrow \text{one upstream continuation}one admissible interior⟶one operational source⟶one conserved mass charge⟶one upstream continuation ⟶two lawful dynamical projections⟶complete target occupancy⟶one certificate orbit.\longrightarrow \text{two lawful dynamical projections} \longrightarrow \text{complete target occupancy} \longrightarrow \text{one certificate orbit}.⟶two lawful dynamical projections⟶complete target occupancy⟶one certificate orbit.

The result is neither an additive juxtaposition of gravity and quantum mechanics nor a declaration that one reduces to the other. It is a dependency-controlled common-origin construction in which both descriptions occupy distinct but coordinated roles inside one complete standing-bearing certificate.

Complete Certificate Closure therefore supplies the terminal theorem of the gravity–quantum unification ARC: the accepted Einstein, Schrödinger, source, mass, continuation, record, boundary, and certificate structures integrate without dependency regression into one quotient-faithful FEP-1.0 realization.

Place in the Completed ARC

The completed gravity–quantum sequence is:



Gravity as Structural Necessity — establishes the necessary physical constraint role;

Einstein Dynamics — derives the unique minimal first-response metric projection;

Schrödinger Dynamics — derives the corresponding minimal Hamiltonian quantum projection;

Structural Unification — proves that the gravitational and quantum descriptions are projections of one admissible physical interior;

Module A — completes operational metric–source attachment.

Module B — proves common mass–charge matching.

Module C — constructs common upstream continuation and projection intertwining.

Module D — establishes exact certificate-fibre exhaustion and unique lawful role occupancy.

Complete Certificate Closure — integrates the full FEP-1.0 gravity–quantum realization certificate.


The capstone is therefore the endpoint of the completed ARC rather than another standalone local module.

Scope and Nonclaims

The manuscript does not claim:



an independent rederivation of the universal AASC kernel;

that Einstein and Schrödinger dynamics are mathematically identical;

reduction of gravity to quantum mechanics or quantum mechanics to gravity;

a new mixed Einstein–Schrödinger field equation;

unrestricted uniqueness across different targets, source packages, sectors, topologies, boundary conditions, or calibrations;

raw uniqueness of all mathematical presentations;

quantization of the Einstein metric as a primitive step;

construction of a Wheeler–DeWitt equation;

a nonzero interacting quantum-gravity field theory;

recovery of every conventional quantum-gravity formalism;

derivation of all Standard Model fields, masses, or couplings;

global existence through every singular or nonregular regime;

elimination of gauge, phase, chart, or representative freedom;

that reversible evolution alone produces irreversible records;

that certificate integration can replace missing standalone proofs;

closure beyond the exact theorem interfaces and frozen FEP-1.0 comparison class.


The theorem is complete relative to its declared target, imports, regularity domain, calibration package, and certificate-equivalence relation.

SYM-5 of Symmetry ARC

 

A Target-Fibered Orbit–Stabilizer–Invariant and Role-Occupancy Closure

This paper develops a target-indexed classification of Standard Model flavor structure that keeps distinct six objects often conflated in flavor physics:



the zero-Yukawa kinetic flavor action;

weak-basis redescription;

the stabilizer of a fixed Yukawa or mass-tensor point;

spurion covariance;

quotient-stable mass, mixing, and CPCP invariants;

anomaly and higher-group data.


The central mathematical object is the flavor action groupoid

YD//GDbasis,\mathcal{Y}_D // G_D^{\mathrm{basis}},

formed separately for the minimal zero-Yukawa Standard Model, generic and singular quark sectors, minimal massless-neutrino and Dirac-neutrino targets, Weinberg/Majorana targets, quark and minimal-lepton Minimal Flavor Violation effective theories, and a higher-flavor interface target.

The paper proves that the physical flavor symmetry at a fixed tensor point is the stabilizer of that point, not the complete covariance group. Generic parameter counts are derived from tensor-space dimension, basis-group dimension, and stabilizer dimension. Registered singular strata are recomputed independently rather than inherited from generic CKM or PMNS charts.

The analysis provides target-specific treatments of:



the maximal zero-Yukawa flavor action and its faithful global qualification;

weak-basis transformations and presentation quotients;

fixed-point stabilizers and factorization isotropy;

generic and singular quark flavor structure;

CKM mixing and quark-sector CPCP invariants;

massless, Dirac, and Majorana neutrino flavor targets;

generalized CPCP conditions;

spurion covariance and conditional Minimal Flavor Violation;

invariant algebras and completeness at generic and singular strata;

rank loss, degeneracy, enhanced stabilizers, and phase survival;

higher-flavor and global-form interfaces.


Spurions are treated as nondynamical backgrounds that encode symmetry-breaking ancestry and organize effective-operator structure. They are not promoted into primitive physical objects or independent dynamical symmetries. Likewise, bases, matrix entries, chart phases, and labels do not acquire physical authority merely by being recorded.

The AASC classification is applied only after the framework-neutral quantum-field-theoretic construction is complete. Target roles and physical residues are generated independently. The finite certificate contains:



9 target fibers;

25 registered results;

12 generic and singular strata;

82 independently constructed role positions;

82 independently constructed QFT residues;

778 evaluated same-target role–residue pairs;

1 compatible complete assignment;

1 lawful assignment orbit;

12 successful mutation tests.


The result establishes a unique lawful integrated flavor normal form for the declared SYM-5 target family:

∣OSYM5∣=1.\left|\mathcal{O}_{\mathrm{SYM5}}\right|=1.

This closure is relative to the registered sharp-local and effective-field-theory targets, their stated conventions, and their completeness certificates. It does not predict numerical Yukawa data, derive generation cardinality from matrix size, assert Minimal Flavor Violation as a fact of nature, or close the global-form, line-operator, monopole, defect, and generalized-symmetry sectors assigned to SYM-9. Whole-arc symmetry exhaustion remains reserved for SYM-11.

Principal contributions



A target-fibered Standard Model flavor-symmetry framework.

A rigorous separation of flavor action, basis skin, stabilizer, spurion covariance, and physical invariants.

Action-groupoid and orbit–stabilizer treatment of flavor structure.

Generic and singular quark-sector parameter-count and invariant analysis.

Separate massless, Dirac-neutrino, and Majorana flavor targets.

Target-specific generalized-CPCP criteria.

Spurion no-promotion and conditional MFV theorems.

Invariant-completeness discipline across orbit strata.

Higher-flavor and global-form interface classification.

Independent role and residue construction.

Complete finite role-occupancy closure with lawful automorphism quotient.

Reproducible audit, mutation testing, and explicit theorem boundaries.

 

Primitive Necessity, Exact-Three Occupation, and Nonpromotion under Enrichment

This paper establishes an open-generation theorem for the cardinality of a primitive generation architecture.

The result is derived before empirical family matching, numerical mass values, mixing matrices, or preferred embedded substructures are introduced. The original primitive task is specified with multiplicity open and requires the joint realization of:





inherited representation, charge, and weak-chiral compatibility;




compatible mass-response slots;




direct comparison of generation occurrences;




boundary preparation, propagation, and endpoint reconstruction;




nonvacuous holonomy or phase capacity;




selector independence;




joint realization of the complete duty package.



The central result is:

Complete primitive generation standing at cardinality n ⇔ n = 3.

This is a theorem about primitive role occupancy within the declared open-generation domain. It is not an empirical count of observed families or a dimension count of a downstream matrix.

Primitive Capacity and the Exact-Three Endpoint

The comparison architecture is complete on the candidate occurrence set and is considered modulo vertex-local rephasing.

The paper proves that non-removable comparison capacity first appears on a triple of occurrences:





fewer than three positions have only removable gradient-like comparison data;




every minimal live support is a triangle;




every larger capacity-bearing architecture contains a proper live three-position support;




the minimal bare architecture has one isomorphism type, the complete three-vertex graph;




the associated primitive comparison quotient has rank one.



The rank-one residue is therefore a consequence of the derived three-position architecture. It is not inserted as an independent premise and does not determine the cardinality by itself.

The argument distinguishes ordinary non-removable capacity from reversal-sensitive capacity. A particular realized parameter point may be CP-conserving while the architecture retains the capacity required by the primitive task. Architectural capacity and the status of an individual physical parameter point are separate questions.

Joint Realization of Primitive Duties

A nonzero comparison residue alone is not sufficient. The primitive work must be realized jointly.

The paper constructs a common receiving packet that simultaneously supports:





the inherited matter and charge representations;




weak-chiral and bridge compatibility;




charged-response slots;




direct comparison and phase quotient data;




coherent boundary preparation and endpoint readout;




nonvacuous holonomy;




selector-independent role formation;




joint compatibility and failure records.



The construction is uniform over the admitted finite phase classes. It uses one common parameter packet for the declared primitive duties rather than proving each duty separately in incompatible realizations.

This establishes both directions of the endpoint:





no fewer than three positions can perform the complete primitive work;




a three-position architecture can perform it jointly.



The count is therefore a constructional minimum and a forced endpoint, not a fitted parameter or an observed multiplicity.

Role Formation and Complete Occupation

The primitive role template is formed from the minimal adequate architecture before candidate residues, observed families, or downstream matching are considered.

For a live candidate architecture, all compatible minimal preassignments are retained. Their live union defines the primitive occupant set. Complete occupation requires:





a compatible assignment from the derived role template;




coverage of the entire live primitive union;




no omitted live occupant;




no added primitive role;




one joint physical receipt for the complete assignment;




lawful transport under role-incidence permutations.



The paper proves:

ClosedGenCertₚ₀(n) ⇔ n = 3.

At the endpoint, the three primitive positions are homogeneous occurrences of one derived architecture. They are not three different unary work-types, and the theorem does not depend on choosing one preferred embedded triangle.

Alternative role assignments form one lawful role-incidence orbit up to permutation. This gives uniqueness of the compatible role occupation at the architectural level without claiming that all physical parameter values, phase assignments, or microscopic realizations are identical.

Nonpromotion under Enrichment

A central theorem excludes primitive promotion by downstream enlargement.

Additional responses, interactions, matrix entries, numerical values, executions, or physical inventories may carry legitimate standing within an enriched task. They do not acquire an additional primitive generation role for the unchanged primitive work merely by being present.

For an enlarged architecture with more than three positions, one of two things occurs:





it fails one of the original primitive duties; or




it contains a proper three-position support that already realizes the complete primitive task.



In the second case, the larger architecture is not primitive-minimal. It may represent a richer physical or mathematical system, but its additional positions are not forced primitive roles for the original generation problem.

This distinction is essential:





a primitive carrier is not the same as a downstream physical role carrier;




primitive architecture is not the same as field-copy count;




primitive role occupancy is not the same as matrix dimension;




primitive cardinality is not selected by masses, labels, or fitted textures.



Count Descent without a Preferred Witness

The paper also gives a relational receiving theorem.

An enriched object may receive one or more licensed primitive constructions without containing a canonically selected embedded triangle. If the receipt relation is nonempty, every received primitive construction has the same count:

Nₚᵣᵢₘ = 3.

Thus the primitive cardinality descends through the receiving relation without requiring:





a preferred embedded triangle;




a selector for one witness;




a distinguished orientation;




a unique phase representative;




or a function choosing one primitive receipt from all admissible receipts.



Further original answers descend only when they are constant on the actual receipt fibres. Primitive count has this property automatically; phase values, detailed histories, or other enriched outputs may not.

Grounded Derived Authority

The paper proves that grounded derived constructions preserve the original primitive qualification closure when every added macro expands into an explicit derivation from the original rules.

This permits useful downstream constructions while excluding claims of new primitive authority arising solely from:





later-stage placement;




reclassification;




a derived operator name;




a circular certificate;




or a macro whose premises already contain the conclusion’s only support.



New independent premises may change the enriched task, but they require their own warrant. They do not retroactively alter the primitive count of the original construction.

Scope and Nonclaims

The theorem is established in an open-generation arena in which the primitive occurrence multiplicity is not fixed in advance.

It does not claim:





that the observed number of fermion families is itself an empirical proof of the theorem;




that masses, mixing angles, CKM or PMNS entries, oscillation data, anomaly data, or fitted textures select the count;




that the theorem derives every downstream Standard Model parameter;




that the primitive carrier is identical to every physical field or enriched receiver;




that a larger enriched theory is impossible;




that every larger carrier lacks legitimate physical roles;




that the theorem identifies a unique microscopic realization;




that a preferred embedded triangle or phase representative must exist;




that the paper replaces the domain-specific receiving proof required by a downstream application.



A fourth generation or larger architecture would need to demonstrate a same-scope primitive role-occupancy certificate for the unchanged duty package. Merely adding downstream structure is not such a certificate.

Role in the AASC Programme

This paper supplies the generation-cardinality endpoint of the AASC carrier and role-occupancy programme:

kernel necessity → primitive duty package → non-removable comparison capacity → joint three-position realization → forced role template → complete occupation → nonpromotion under enrichment.

Its conclusion is structural and constructional:



The complete primitive generation work has exactly three primitive role positions, and downstream enrichment cannot add further primitive authority for that unchanged work.


The result provides a governance-level generation endpoint for receiving constructions while preserving the distinction between primitive role occupancy, enriched physical content, and the detailed values realized within a particular physical system.

